```javascript
function rotateString(str) {
    // Check if the string is empty or has less than 3 characters
    if (str.length <= 3) {
        return str;
    }

    // Calculate the new starting index after rotation
    const startIndex = str.length - 3;

    // Slice the string into two parts
    const rotatedPart = str.slice(startIndex);
    const remainingPart = str.slice(0, startIndex);

    // Concatenate the rotated part with the remaining part
    const rotatedString = rotatedPart + remainingPart;

    return rotatedString;
}

// Example usage
const inputString = "abcdef";
const result = rotateString(inputString);
console.log(result); // Output: "defabc"
```

This function first checks if the input string is empty or has fewer than 3 characters. If so, it simply returns the original string, as there's no meaningful rotation possible. For strings with 3 or more characters, it calculates the starting index for the rotated part, which is the length of the string minus 3. It then uses the `slice` method to extract the part of the string that will be moved to the beginning and the part that remains. Finally, it combines these parts to form the rotated string and returns it.

